WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.500
COLLIN FRIERSON:
Welcome to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.840
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.910
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.160
My name is Collin
Frierson, and today we're

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.460
going to be talking
about G Suite Security.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.860
We're going to look at
MDM, Enterprise Security

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.540
and Protection and more.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.050
You can ask questions
any time on the platform.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.930
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.400
With that, let's get started.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.050
One of the things
we know is it's

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.980
becoming increasingly difficult
for IT to manage and protect

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.780
assets at scale.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.839
The average IT
organization has thousands

00:00:56.839 --> 00:00:58.380
of services they're
trying to manage,

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.420
both on prem and in Cloud.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.100
And a high percentage
of data breaches

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.340
are caused by employees, due
to a lack of internal controls.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.950
Meaning it's very
difficult to govern

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.510
when employees
have access to data

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.650
and when they don't, and
where that data goes.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.500
We're also seeing
that patchwork systems

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.970
makes the system as a
whole much more vulnerable.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.890
Meaning you're going to
have varying operating

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.980
systems on prem, varying
patch levels on those systems.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:31.080
And those varying patch levels
can be exploited by documents,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.540
by malicious code that are
often delivered via email.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:40.420
And the costs of these
breaches is going up.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.970
The number of breaches we've
seen is actually increasing.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.410
And data breaches
can be catastrophic.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.439
We understand this
really well at Google,

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:50.980
and we've been
designing our products

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.560
to try to address
these concerns.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.420
Some of the things
we've noticed--

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.170
90% plus of phishing
attacks start with email.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.010
Similarly with malware, we
see that 66% or maybe even

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.470
a little more of
malware is actually

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.470
delivered via email or
an attachment via email.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.010
So it's really important we
defend that from the beginning.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.270
And again, a very high
percentage of reported breaches

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.450
are caused by either employees
not understanding where

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.330
the proper boundaries
are, being extorted,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.450
or by an external threat.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.490
And data exfiltration
is expensive.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.030
We're seeing a super high
increase in the losses

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.350
from ransomware over the
last couple of years.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.300
So at Google, we really believe
that being in in a cloud native

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.700
can really help you
defend against these types

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:43.630
of modern threats.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.290
There's some
amazing capabilities

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.210
that work across the platform,
whether you're in the Google

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.630
Drive ecosystem directly
or in Gmail or using

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.120
Chrome Enterprise.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.360
Our robust global
infrastructure is designed

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.010
to always be up-to-date.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.430
So no one is ever running
an old version of Gmail.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.510
We deploy security patches to
Chrome on a very regular basis.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.150
And the infrastructure is
global so that we minimize

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.090
the amount of time your
traffic is actually on the web,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.570
and we bring it into
Google as fast as possible.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:18.210
So as we're building
products inside of G Suite,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.800
we think about it in a
couple of different buckets--

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.360
proactive, intelligent,
compliant, and simple.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.070
So what I'm going to
do today is take you

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.640
through each of these areas
of work inside of G Suite.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.130
And then please feel
free to ask questions,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.960
and we can dive into
any area that we

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.850
need to do a little bit more.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.820
Proactive, I think, is one
of the most important ones.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:44.310
Again, we see a ton of
spam and phishing campaigns

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.050
that come directly
in from email.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:49.980
So being defensive at
email is really important.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:56.910
And Gmail is very effective
at stopping spam and malware.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.000
We have three 9s here, which
is really, really impressive,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.080
and this is hard to do.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.610
The 99.9%, the way we're
able to achieve that,

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.307
is actually through
Machine Learning.

00:04:10.307 --> 00:04:12.640
I'm going to talk a little
bit more about this in a bit,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.900
but this is how we're able to
classify spam and malware not

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.250
just based on signature
or by sender reputation.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.120
We have a lot of signals
that we're able to use.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.980
And we also classify
it in multiple points

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.710
during message delivery.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.210
A couple of other
interesting things

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.869
we're doing with
Machine Learning.

00:04:28.869 --> 00:04:29.910
These are some new stats.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.700
We actually just published
these a couple of weeks ago.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:36.300
Every minute, we're at 10
million spam messages prevented

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.520
from reaching a Gmail inbox.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.560
10 million per minute.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.539
This is a huge number.

00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:44.580
And again, this is based
on that Machine Learning

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.730
with 99.9% accuracy rates.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.520
Over half a million
pages indexed and scanned

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.700
for harmful software.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.700
So this is using the
Google Search engine.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.460
We're actually looking
for malicious sites--

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.632
sites that host malware,
links that might

00:04:58.632 --> 00:05:00.840
be in your email that would
direct you to a page that

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.060
would do something harmful.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:06.390
7,000 deceitful URLs,
browser extensions

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.970
that may carry viruses, unwanted
content, phishing attempts.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.360
Again, we're detecting
7,000 new ones per minute.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.092
And we're also scanning 6,000
instances of software that

00:05:17.092 --> 00:05:18.300
could be malicious to Chrome.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.550
These are binaries
that are hosted,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.870
and when you download
them, Chrome would warn you

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.870
that this binary may harm
your computer, are you

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.020
sure you want to keep it.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.766
And every minute, two phishing
sites and one malware site

00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:34.140
are found and
labeled, so they're

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.200
added to the Chrome
Safe Browsing database.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.210
This is only possible
with Google Search.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.800
The search engine
and Machine Learning

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:41.883
really make this possible.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.840
And talking more about
the email accuracy,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.704
there's a lot of vendors in this
space that do email detection.

00:05:52.704 --> 00:05:54.120
What we really
think sets us apart

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.520
is the Machine Learning
that is individualized.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.070
So meaning not only are we
able to detect ransomware,

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.390
detect messages
there will morph--

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.890
so we'll see these messages try
to beat signature detection,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.514
they'll send millions
of emails and try

00:06:06.514 --> 00:06:09.700
to look to see which ones
are the most effective.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.685
But we're also able to
look for things also

00:06:11.685 --> 00:06:13.310
that are in the Safe
Browsing database.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.630
So when you attach
a link, Google

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.040
will look at where
the link goes.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.990
And then if it's sending
you to a malicious page,

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.406
we'll stop you
from getting there.

00:06:20.406 --> 00:06:23.460
We'll even strip the link out.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.800
And also, this doesn't just
apply to inbound messages.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.760
This applies to
outbound as well.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.270
So we actually will look
for sensitive information

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.460
that you as a G Suite
administrator can configure

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.410
with our Data Loss
Prevention engine

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.230
and then detect if content that
is leaving the organization

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.270
meets compliance rules.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.821
So you can say, look,
if you're sending emails

00:06:41.821 --> 00:06:44.070
with a lot of swear words
in them, I could block that.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.740
If you're sending emails
with sensitive information

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.834
like credit card numbers,
names, addresses,

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:50.250
once it meets a
certain threshold,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.920
you can warn, block, or
change routing rules.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.287
The email layers of
defenses are really cool,

00:06:58.287 --> 00:06:59.370
and there's a lot of them.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:02.600
So for example, we're doing
a lot of checks pre-delivery.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:06.740
And this is partly how
we get to that 99.9%.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:11.570
As a message comes into Gmail,
we look not only for rule-based

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:16.190
matching-- so did it come from a
sender with a poor reputation--

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.470
we also are able to even
open any attachments

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.952
in a virtual machine
and essentially see

00:07:20.952 --> 00:07:22.160
what they did to the machine.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:24.590
So we'll dump the memory,
we'll look at the disk

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:28.320
and determine really quickly,
usually inside of four minutes,

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.880
if that binary is malicious.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.995
This is really how we're
looking for zero days.

00:07:31.995 --> 00:07:34.370
And then of course, we'll use
multiple anti-virus engines

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.250
to look for known signatures--

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:40.970
if there's a zip file with
contents in it, we'll open it,

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.060
we'll extract it all, and
we'll see what's in there.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.160
We also look again
on Message Open.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.770
So we rerun a lot of these
checks on Message Open.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:51.720
We know that email's
happening in real time.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.680
So even post-delivery,
we can check

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.640
to see did the
links in this site

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.830
match a new Safe
Browsing data entry.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.100
We can display warning
banners inside of Gmail.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:07.310
And going even further,
when you open an attachment,

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.770
if you're opening it
in the Gmail web UI,

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.810
that's actually a sandbox
where we're actually

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:12.770
opening the attachment.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.660
So it's really difficult for
traditional macros or PDF

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:18.680
exploits--

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.730
for those to execute inside the
Chrome sandbox inside of Gmail.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:26.120
Again, looking at Safe
Browsing links on link clicks--

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.560
so before you click
on anything, we're

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.476
going to check for the
Safe Browsing database,

00:08:29.476 --> 00:08:31.940
about where you're
about to be headed.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.870
We're also sending
things like Reply.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.260
So if someone is
impersonating your domain--

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.904
so they've spun up a domain
that's very similar to yours,

00:08:38.904 --> 00:08:40.820
they have a character
or two that's different,

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:42.679
and they're asking
for information--

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:44.720
Google will automatically
warn the user that hey,

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:45.990
you're about to send
information that's

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.390
sensitive outside the domain.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.970
Are you sure you
want to do that?

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.027
Again, also doing data
loss prevention checks

00:08:52.027 --> 00:08:54.110
when you're doing an OAuth
grant-- so if someone's

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:57.200
trying to ask you to click
on a link that authorizes

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.740
a third-party application to
your G Suite applications,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.950
we can actually look at DLP
before we send any information

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.960
to that third party.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.480
Finally, some of the best
ones are password verification

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.320
and password reset.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:14.660
So we'll do 2-step verification
when we see suspicious login

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.220
attempts.

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.729
And we can also challenge
when someone's sending you

00:09:18.729 --> 00:09:21.020
password reset emails, we'll
make sure those are really

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.610
coming from secure sources.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:26.360
I think it's also
important to acknowledge

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.850
that we can interlock a
lot of these protections

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.920
with Chrome OS.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.160
Chrome OS is one of the
most secure computing

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.410
platforms on the market today.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.781
And we're not only combining
all of those capabilities

00:09:38.781 --> 00:09:40.280
that we just talked
about, but we're

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.880
combining it with a secure
foundation for the client.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.580
Meaning the device,
the firmware,

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.190
the OS are all designed
with security in mind.

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.780
So a couple of examples here.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.420
The first one is
every Chromebook

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:57.435
on the market is fully
encrypted, that's by design.

00:09:57.435 --> 00:09:59.810
There isn't a Chromebook on
the market today you can find

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.330
that isn't it fully encrypted.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:05.990
And actually, we're
encrypting the user space.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.660
The OS itself is
actually open source,

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.510
so there's no need
to encrypt that.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.690
The firmware is also
designed to have secure boot,

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:18.260
meaning every time
Chrome OS boots,

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:22.110
we actually check to make sure
the OS is intact with firmware.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.887
And if something's
wrong, you'll rarely

00:10:23.887 --> 00:10:25.970
see that screen that says,
hey, something's wrong.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:30.020
You need to insert a USB drive
and reimage your Chromebook.

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.030
And again, the OS platform
is managed by Google.

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:35.354
So we're delivering security
updates every six weeks.

00:10:35.354 --> 00:10:37.520
Or if you want to opt into
the beta or dev channels,

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.870
you can get updates even faster.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.720
But again, you won't
find Chromebooks

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.678
that are running really
old versions of Chrome.

00:10:43.678 --> 00:10:45.740
We automatically
push these updates.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.440
And we can really stay ahead
of the curve with patching.

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:54.080
All right, with that, let's
talk about intelligence.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:57.380
So today we actually have
three billion devices

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:00.440
that are checked with
Safe Browsing technology.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.860
And we're, again, scanning
the entire internet

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:08.300
for harmful software
and for malicious sites.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.640
And we want to make all
of this data available.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.650
So one of the tools we
just launched in G Suite

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:15.440
is called Security Center.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.540
Security Center is a
place where administrators

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:21.742
can go to look for
data on everything that

00:11:21.742 --> 00:11:24.200
would be relevant to someone
who's a security professional.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.290
t how many phishing emails
am I getting in my domain?

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:29.184
Is it trending up
or trending down?

00:11:29.184 --> 00:11:31.100
How many of them are
blocked, how many of them

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.620
are blocked retroactively?

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.690
How many links am I getting
that contain malware?

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.180
How many attachments
contain malware?

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.520
And then even data on how
the users are behaving.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:43.370
How many OAuth links
is a user creating?

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:44.960
What applications
have they authorized

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.420
to see data inside their domain?

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:50.780
You can retroactively
go back and revoke.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:52.280
Finally, in Security
Center, I think

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:55.970
one of the coolest features
is a security assistant

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.230
that we have.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:58.790
It actually provides
recommendations

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:02.600
and best practices for
security in the domain.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.630
And you can see
recommendations like, hey,

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:07.912
if you have your
super admins and they

00:12:07.912 --> 00:12:09.870
don't have two-factor
authentication turned on,

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.532
you should turn that on.

00:12:12.532 --> 00:12:14.240
And you can accept
those recommendations,

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:16.950
or you can say I
accept the risk.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:20.990
It's live, so it will interact
with you as you go through it.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:25.230
Really important tool, I
think, to take a look at.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:28.590
The second one is
Data Loss Prevention.

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.720
This tool has been around
in Gmail for a while.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.010
We launched it in
Drive late last year.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.230
So DLP and Drive
will actually let

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:38.850
you look for sensitive
content across

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.650
your entire organization.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.500
Meaning when you're looking for
things like names, credit card

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:44.370
numbers--

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:47.010
sensitive data that would likely
be the source of a breach,

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.730
you can apply rules
programmatically to that data.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.320
What that means is
you can allow users

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:55.080
to share, so long as
the content isn't filled

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.390
with names or credit card
numbers or social security

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:58.140
numbers.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:00.482
We can hold all that on
Google Platform, by the way.

00:13:00.482 --> 00:13:02.190
We have lots of
compliance certifications

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.357
showing that we can do that.

00:13:03.357 --> 00:13:05.481
But you want to make sure
that your users are still

00:13:05.481 --> 00:13:07.710
behaving in a way that's
governed by the security

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.060
policy.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.510
This is actually a tool that
was built by Google for itself.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.170
We actually built this as part
of a log sanitization engine.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:19.620
So it's trained with Machine
Learning, it runs at scale

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.601
and operates, actually,
in a very similar way

00:13:22.601 --> 00:13:23.850
that our Gmail scanning works.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:26.340
Every time someone makes
an edit, DLP reruns,

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.710
re-evaluate the rules.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.080
So it works on live
documents, and of course, it

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.630
works even with
non-Google native objects,

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:35.880
like Word documents, PDFs,
images, things like that.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.260
So with that, let's talk
briefly about compliance.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:46.940
So at Google, we have a
pretty simple philosophy

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.260
when it comes to compliance.

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:50.270
And that is we should
find the strongest

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:53.030
technical certifications
and attestations we

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:58.650
can find globally and
adhere to those standards.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:01.550
So this has been a huge
body of work for Google.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.820
And we've been looking at
things like the ISO series.

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:10.400
27001 is your standard
Cloud security attestation.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:14.990
27017 is the list of all
those technical controls.

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:18.470
And ISO 27018 is actually
the Cloud privacy standard,

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.980
so it makes sure that the data
that you put inside of G Suite

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.590
is private from both a
multi-tendency perspective,

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.172
it's private from the host,
which would be Google,

00:14:27.172 --> 00:14:28.880
and private from any
third-parties Google

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:29.671
does business with.

00:14:29.671 --> 00:14:31.490
So it's a really
strong standard,

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:34.250
I'm really happy we got
that a couple of years ago.

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.070
We also offer SOC reports.

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:39.290
So if you need an SSAE
report or a Type 2 report,

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:41.180
we can definitely provide that.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:44.742
And G Suite is FedRAMP
certified at the moderate level.

00:14:44.742 --> 00:14:46.450
So this has also been
around for a while.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:49.070
But again, it attests to
the strength of the controls

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:51.590
that we have in place.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.850
We're also gearing towards
regulatory compliance,

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.447
so things like the US
health care with HIPAA.

00:14:57.447 --> 00:14:59.030
So we want to make
sure that if you're

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:01.250
storing health-related
information in G Suite,

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:03.950
we can protect that
data adequately.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:06.210
Also similarly with
GDPR in the EU.

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:08.630
So we want to make sure that
even the data in G Suite

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:11.270
is held to the highest
privacy standards,

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:15.060
and the EU is really
leading on this.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.340
It's worth mentioning, we offer
this to all of our customers.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.220
We don't differentiate
between the editions.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:23.280
So regardless of what G
Suite edition you're in,

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.510
we're going to offer these to
you no matter where you are.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.190
A couple other
commitments-- there's

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.500
never any ads in G Suite.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.330
Google does not
collect your data.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:34.720
We don't scan it
for advertising.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.210
We don't create archetypes.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.149
We don't do any of
that in G Suite.

00:15:38.149 --> 00:15:39.690
You don't have to
believe me on this.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.280
It's in our terms of
service for G Suite.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.613
So you can look at the contract.

00:15:42.613 --> 00:15:45.330
It's very, very clear.

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:46.222
You own the data.

00:15:46.222 --> 00:15:47.430
This is also in the contract.

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:50.400
Google commits that we have
no intellectual property

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.380
rights on any of the
data you put in G Suite.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:56.172
It's 100% yours, and you're also
responsible for the content.

00:15:56.172 --> 00:15:57.630
And your apps are
always available.

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:00.230
We offer a really high SLA.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.370
We also have public
dashboarding to show

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:05.280
the status of our applications.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.380
This SLA includes no
time for maintenance,

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.620
so we're always going
to be at three 9s.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:13.987
We do maintenance all the time,
we do patching all the time.

00:16:13.987 --> 00:16:16.070
So we're always going to
be on the latest version,

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:17.653
and we're never going
to take you down

00:16:17.653 --> 00:16:21.840
for a maintenance window at
some horrible time in the night.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:23.250
G Suite is global.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:26.400
It's always daytime
and working somewhere.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.020
We also really
commit to control.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:31.590
This is really important.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.280
This to me means two things.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:35.670
One thing is you can
always take your data out

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:36.712
of Google if you need to.

00:16:36.712 --> 00:16:38.836
We don't want you to feel
like you're trapped here.

00:16:38.836 --> 00:16:41.310
So if you've been using
Google Docs or Google Slides,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.440
you can always export
that in industry formats

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.254
or open formats.

00:16:45.254 --> 00:16:46.670
And you can also
delete your data.

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:47.940
So if you tell
Google, hey, I want

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:49.380
to delete all of
this data, we're

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:51.690
contractually committed
to deleting that for you

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:54.180
and processing that command.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:56.430
And again, we have these
commitments in contract.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:58.596
You can read more about
them in the terms of service

00:16:58.596 --> 00:17:01.810
or in the data
processing amendment.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:02.920
Last one is simple.

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:04.930
All these security
controls are great.

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:07.960
The philosophy here is
if they're not simple

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.256
and it's not very easy
for users to use it,

00:17:10.256 --> 00:17:12.130
typically that's how
work-arounds get created

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.260
and problems occur.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:18.099
So the first one is around
the administration side.

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:20.920
The administrator tools that
are in G Suite's central admin

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.970
panel are really easy to
use and straightforward.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.960
I use the search panel
a lot, the search panel

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:26.710
is really helpful.

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:28.540
And we even support
natural language query

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:29.810
in the search bar.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:31.739
So if you type
password resets, we'll

00:17:31.739 --> 00:17:33.280
understand what that
means, and we'll

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:35.720
direct you to the right place.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.090
We also include tools in
here for Chrome management.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.250
So if you need to
administer Chrome devices,

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:44.770
whether those are signed-in
Chrome devices or Chrome OS

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:47.110
devices, we can
manage this from here.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.270
We include our mobile
device management in here,

00:17:49.270 --> 00:17:52.450
so you can manage iOS
and Android devices.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:54.820
And we also support
Information Rights Management

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:55.760
through this console.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.600
So you can determine
rules for when content

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:01.877
has a certain requirement,
is that content

00:18:01.877 --> 00:18:03.460
allowed to be taken
offline, taken out

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:05.020
of the domain, et cetera.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:06.820
Really powerful.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:09.700
There's also controls
for team drives in here.

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:11.460
One of the new things
we launched recently

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.060
is called Protected
Team Drives, which

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:16.810
will let you actually define
rules about content in a drive

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:20.290
so that content no matter
what can't be taken offline,

00:18:20.290 --> 00:18:23.020
can't be taken
out of the domain.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.620
You can even restrict
copy and print.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:25.495
It's really powerful.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:30.730
Our Mobile Device Management
and Cloud Identity

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:32.360
is also becoming really popular.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:35.950
So we have a ton of devices
now that are under management.

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:38.290
And we've actually
recently simplified

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:41.110
how much weight we
have on the device,

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:44.020
in order to put Mobile
Device Management on there.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:46.930
So we're calling this
MDM light because we

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:49.480
don't need an agent anymore to
enforce basic policies, things

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.760
like encryption and pass codes.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.440
And we also still
support, of course,

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.390
you can push a full agent
and have even more control.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:59.260
This is actually on by
default now for new domains,

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:00.460
which is really exciting.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:02.320
We definitely want to make sure
administrators have control

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:04.700
when users are accessing their
data from a mobile device,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.755
whether it's Android or iOS.

00:19:07.755 --> 00:19:09.630
Finally, I want to spend
a little bit of time

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:12.780
on security keys because this
has been key to even Google's

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.650
corporate strategy.

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:17.190
We, of course, eat
our own dog food.

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:20.730
So we use G Suite tools
and Gmail services, just

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:23.340
like all of our customers.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:26.220
And security keys are
paramount to our strategy

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:29.430
for preventing phishing
attacks and preventing

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.119
account hijacking.

00:19:31.119 --> 00:19:32.910
Remember, one of the
stats I quoted earlier

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:40.950
was over 70% of phishing
campaigns start with an email.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.330
Security keys are another
layer of defense for us there.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:45.420
So suppose a phishing
email gets through,

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:48.330
an employee clicks on the link.

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:50.136
The bar we set with
security keys was we

00:19:50.136 --> 00:19:51.510
want to build a
credential that's

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:55.950
so strong that it's nearly
impossible for a bad actor

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.600
to steal it.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:01.620
Security keys are how Google
has accomplished that.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:03.540
Since we deployed
security keys, we've

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:08.214
had no reported or detected
G Suite account hijackings

00:20:08.214 --> 00:20:08.880
post-deployment.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.201
So that means an employee
who has a security key--

00:20:12.201 --> 00:20:13.950
you know, there have
been lots of attempts

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:15.360
to steal their credential.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.460
None has been successful
that we've detected

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:19.050
or have been reported.

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:20.790
So security keys
work by actually

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:22.696
doing something
very different than

00:20:22.696 --> 00:20:23.820
traditional authentication.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:25.278
Traditional
authentication requires

00:20:25.278 --> 00:20:27.540
you to demonstrate to
the server that you

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:29.160
are who you say you are.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.500
What's fundamentally
different about security keys

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.710
is security keys require
that the server demonstrate

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.820
to the client that
it's trustworthy.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:39.960
So now we're doing
bidirectional authentication.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.724
So when you're authenticating
with a security key,

00:20:42.724 --> 00:20:44.640
the server actually
demonstrates to the client

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.140
that it really is
who it says it is.

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:48.990
You're really in a TLS
connection with the server,

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:50.587
there's not a man in the middle.

00:20:50.587 --> 00:20:52.170
There's not a bad
actor phishing page.

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:53.880
And that credential
that you send

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.160
can't be replayed or reused.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:58.050
We have a great
white paper on this.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:02.640
If you want to know more, I can
tell you about our experience

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.220
with security keys, which
is a public white paper.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:07.605
Or you can learn more about
U2F from the FIDO alliance.

00:21:10.766 --> 00:21:12.140
So all that being
said, we really

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:15.050
believe that our strength
is better together.

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:18.350
Combining the strength of G
Suite with Chrome and Android

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:21.770
and even GCP is really going
to help you build a security

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:24.950
framework that is extended
beyond the traditional

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.840
perimeter.

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:30.290
As employees become more
mobile, as identities really

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:33.710
start to span outside of
the traditional premiere,

00:21:33.710 --> 00:21:37.370
we think we're building the
most powerfully secure Cloud

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:42.260
platform you can
adopt out there.

00:21:42.260 --> 00:21:45.800
Tying it all together, we
want default-on mobile device

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.150
management.

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:49.010
So we want to be
able to say users

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:52.940
who are signing in and accessing
Google data from mobile device

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:56.180
managed services are going
to be following basic rules,

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:59.450
like is my device
encrypted, do I

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:03.400
have a passcode on my lock
screen, things like that.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.840
Advanced anti-phishing
capabilities, so again, this

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:08.750
is when we're using Chrome,
any link we click on,

00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:11.190
any email that we
get, any attachment

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.710
that's delivered to our
mailbox is going to be scanned.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.460
We're going to be
looking for bad behavior

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:19.050
and blocking that by
the end user by default.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:21.190
Deploying security keys
to the organization.

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:23.725
So we're doing the
strongest 2FA possible.

00:22:23.725 --> 00:22:26.100
And we're making sure that
users who are coming in really

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.150
are who they say
they are, and they're

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:29.070
authenticating to
the real servers

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:33.270
and not to hijacked
or malicious places.

00:22:33.270 --> 00:22:37.050
Using the Security Console in
G Suite and DLP to make sure

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:39.030
the rules of the
organization are followed,

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:40.680
and content is where
it belongs and not

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:44.770
being shared either
accidentally or maliciously.

00:22:44.770 --> 00:22:46.770
And then finally, using
a tool like Google Vault

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.747
to assure legal compliance
with all of the data

00:22:49.747 --> 00:22:51.330
that you're putting
in G Suite, so you

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.750
can support things
like eDiscovery.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:57.820
With that, I definitely
want to thank everyone.

00:22:57.820 --> 00:23:01.300
Stay tuned for live Q&amp;A. And
we will be back in less than a

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:01.800
minute.

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:37.959
All right, we're
back with questions.

00:24:37.959 --> 00:24:39.750
So a couple of questions
from the audience.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:41.610
Do I need a separate
security key

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:44.130
for each account I log into?

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.290
This is a great question.

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:49.440
Security keys, the relationship
between keys and accounts

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.400
is actually many to many.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:54.240
So for any particular
Google account,

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:58.650
you can actually register a very
large number of security keys

00:24:58.650 --> 00:25:00.900
with that account, so you
don't have to have just one,

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.700
in case you lose it.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:05.670
And that key can actually be
used to unlock many accounts.

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:10.470
So in my case, my
one security key

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:13.320
will unlock my corporate
account, my personal Gmail.com

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:17.130
account as well as a bunch
of other demo accounts.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.530
And it even works for other
third party sites, sites

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:23.010
like GitHub, Facebook, Dropbox.

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:24.700
They all support
security keys natively.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:27.630
So you don't need to get a
separate key for each service.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.890
And you can have multiple
keys for your services,

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:33.090
so you don't have to worry
about losing your one key.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:35.250
Does Google encrypt
data at rest?

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:36.762
Yes, we do.

00:25:36.762 --> 00:25:38.220
Google actually
encrypts everything

00:25:38.220 --> 00:25:43.230
by default with no action
required by the administrator.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:45.390
We have a great white
paper on this that talks

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:48.400
about how we encrypt data.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:51.000
So if you want to learn
more, search for G Suite data

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.490
encryption, and we'll tell
you how we encrypt data

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.749
both in transit and at rest.

00:25:55.749 --> 00:25:57.540
The great thing is you
can't turn this off.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:01.050
So it's always going to be on,
and G Suite is really fast.

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:04.470
So it's a great, great
feature of the platform.

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:07.980
How does Google vault and DLP
work with the new Gmail UI

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:10.290
confidential mode?

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:12.510
Vault and DLP
functionality don't change.

00:26:12.510 --> 00:26:16.690
So DLP scans run
on send or receive.

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:19.435
So DLP runs any time you're
sending or receiving a message.

00:26:19.435 --> 00:26:21.060
So even if you're in
Confidential mode,

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:23.280
we will still run DLP checks.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.030
And Google vaults
works the same way.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:29.340
We will still vault
and archive messages

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:33.480
regardless of whether
using confidential or not.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:36.420
Are DLP and Security Center
are available for all G

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:40.490
Suite for business
or only Enterprise?

00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:42.780
This is a good question.

00:26:42.780 --> 00:26:45.300
We're always moving
the addition things.

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:51.346
I think Security Center and
DLP are in Enterprise for sure.

00:26:51.346 --> 00:26:53.220
I'm going to have to
check on whether they're

00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:54.344
in the business SKU or not.

00:26:54.344 --> 00:26:56.100
I don't remember.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:59.957
I think some customers that
were grandfathered on business

00:26:59.957 --> 00:27:02.040
before we launched Enterprise
probably have access

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:03.810
to some of these features.

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:05.190
I'm not an expert on SKUs.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.960
I apologize.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.150
Does Drive DLP
scan current files

00:27:09.150 --> 00:27:11.070
or only ones that
have subsequently

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.140
been uploaded or created?

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.640
Drive DLP scans everything.

00:27:15.640 --> 00:27:18.840
So when you first
launch, a Drive DLP rule

00:27:18.840 --> 00:27:22.450
will actually scan everything
in the organization.

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:23.580
So if you set a policy--

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.530
if it's your first
policy, you'll

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:27.960
see a pop-up that
says it may take up

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.665
to 24 hours for this
rule to take effect.

00:27:30.665 --> 00:27:32.040
But that's because
we're scanning

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:34.990
all of the shares
inside the organization.

00:27:34.990 --> 00:27:38.860
So we're looking across
any existing shares.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:41.040
And again, once the
rule is enacted,

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.460
we're looking on every edit.

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:45.986
So if I have a bunch of
confidential information

00:27:45.986 --> 00:27:47.610
and I'm sharing a
document with someone

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.920
and I paste all that
information into the document,

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:51.900
DLP will run as part
of that and then

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:56.190
subsequently change my sharing
settings as a result of that.

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:58.800
So even if I'm in a live
document, and I'm typing.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.730
As soon as the keystrokes I type
create a social security number

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:05.130
or whatever the DLP
rule will detect,

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.710
it will block and change
that sharing setting.

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:11.270
So it works on everything.

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:14.330
All right, is that all
the questions we have?

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:15.560
Yeah, OK.

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:17.390
Thanks so much for watching.

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:22.700
Tune in next Tuesday for
another edition of Cloud OnAir.

